From 382092d96591e4c819887914a57f038624043928 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:54:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] docs(passes): record the if-combine profitability
 investigation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The short-circuit if-combine rejects the ASCII classifier loop with
"branch in block N survives" even though three of its four links fold.
The cause is that the profitability pre-filter probes the UNMODIFIED IR:
with `||` chains, two links share a join block, so neither converts until
its neighbours have, and `plan_shared_arm_dup` refuses the chained shape.
Making the test a real simulation, and relaxing `skip_boolean_diamond`
inside the simulated loop, does let the fold happen.

It was measured and reverted: on a branchless-friendly reduction the
combined form is not faster. Callgrind on tests/bench/k_classify.c (200
driver passes) gives 19,429,132 instructions with the fold against
19,429,466 without it -- no change -- because the short-circuit form LCCC
already emits costs one predictable branch there, not two mispredicts.
On k_namechars.c the fold is a 33% LOSS (16,786,491 vs 12,575,463): it
converts six short-circuitable compares into six unconditional ones plus
setcc chains. Both numbers contradict the profitability premise for this
compiler's backend, so the conservative gate stays and the measurement is
recorded here instead of a speculative transform.

The real ceilings are now measured and named:
* classify/namechars are 4.4x behind GCC because GCC vectorises the byte
  classification (vpcmpgtb ×6 + vpand/vpor + vpsubb + vpmovmskb, 32 bytes
  per iteration) and LCCC's map vectorizer does not recognise a byte
  classification with a scalar accumulate;
* sqlite_varint_scan is 1.67x behind because GCC inlines get_varint32
  (21 blocks) and propagates the constant `avail`, unrolling the decode;
  LCCC's static-loop inliner caps at 16 blocks. Admitting it was measured
  neutral (+0.000% over the 22-kernel Callgrind corpus) and reverted.
